1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:14,547
In the spring of 1945, as Germany collapsed, a general on the army's intelligence staff had his most valuable files packed into watertight drums and buried in the Alps.

2
00:00:15,647 --> 00:00:21,641
They held much of what Germany knew about the Soviet army. They were his insurance policy.

3
00:00:22,741 --> 00:00:34,987
Within a year he was working for the Americans. For the next twenty-two years, he ran West Germany's foreign intelligence, first for the United States and then for the new West German state.

4
00:00:36,087 --> 00:00:49,420
Reinhard Gehlen is not infamous for killing anyone. He is infamous for what his career revealed: how quickly the West made use of men who had served Hitler, once the enemy became the Soviet Union.

5
00:00:51,620 --> 00:01:05,465
Gehlen was born in Erfurt, in central Germany, in 1902. In 1920, at eighteen, he joined the small army that the Treaty of Versailles allowed Germany to keep.

6
00:01:06,565 --> 00:01:16,848
Quiet, hard-working and ambitious, he rose into the general staff, the army's planning elite. He was a career officer, never a Nazi Party official.

7
00:01:17,948 --> 00:01:27,612
He served as a staff officer during the invasions of Poland in 1939 and France in 1940.

8
00:01:28,712 --> 00:01:38,227
In June 1941, Germany invaded the Soviet Union. Gehlen was working on the planning staff of the army high command.

9
00:01:40,427 --> 00:01:56,213
In April 1942 he took over Foreign Armies East, the army's intelligence department for the Soviet front. It built up a picture of the enemy from captured documents, radio intercepts and the questioning of prisoners.

10
00:01:57,313 --> 00:02:07,468
He was good at it. He reorganised the department, brought in specialists, and turned scattered reports into clear, detailed estimates of Soviet strength.

11
00:02:08,568 --> 00:02:25,528
Those prisoners were held in appalling conditions. Of about five point seven million Soviet soldiers captured by Germany, historians estimate around three point three million died. Gehlen's department did not run the camps, but it drew on that system.

12
00:02:26,628 --> 00:02:44,271
As the Soviet army advanced, Gehlen's reports grew gloomier, and more accurate. In January 1945, according to General Heinz Guderian, Hitler dismissed his estimate of Soviet strength as the greatest imposture since Genghis Khan.

13
00:02:45,371 --> 00:02:55,717
The Soviet offensive came as Gehlen had predicted, and swept across Poland towards Berlin. In April 1945, Hitler sacked him.

14
00:02:57,917 --> 00:03:04,403
With a few loyal officers, he retreated to a remote mountain hut and waited for the war to end.

15
00:03:05,503 --> 00:03:16,063
On the twenty-second of May 1945, he surrendered to the US Army and offered it his files, his expertise and his network of agents.

16
00:03:17,163 --> 00:03:28,021
At first, the Americans barely knew who he was. But their wartime alliance with the Soviet Union was already cracking, and they knew very little about the Red Army.

17
00:03:29,121 --> 00:03:39,404
That summer he was flown to the United States and questioned at a secret interrogation centre at Fort Hunt, Virginia. The Americans liked what they heard.

18
00:03:41,604 --> 00:03:57,583
In 1946 he was back in Germany, running a spy service paid for by the United States. It became known simply as the Gehlen Organization, or the Org. In 1949 the CIA took it over.

19
00:03:58,683 --> 00:04:11,461
As the Cold War deepened, with the Soviet blockade of Berlin in 1948, the Org became one of America's main sources on Soviet forces in East Germany.

20
00:04:12,561 --> 00:04:20,327
Its agents watched railways, airfields and barracks across East Germany, counting Soviet troops, tanks and aircraft.

21
00:04:21,427 --> 00:04:36,659
To staff it, Gehlen turned to people he knew: former army officers, but also former members of the SS, its intelligence arm the SD, and the Gestapo. Some had taken part in Nazi crimes in the occupied east.

22
00:04:37,759 --> 00:04:49,300
Many American officials knew about these men. Some objected. But in the early Cold War, experience against the Soviet Union was valued above almost anything else.

23
00:04:51,500 --> 00:05:03,319
In 1956 the Org became West Germany's official foreign intelligence service, the Bundesnachrichtendienst, or BND. Gehlen was its first president.

24
00:05:04,419 --> 00:05:19,501
He worked closely with the chancellor, Konrad Adenauer. He was hardly alone in having a past: Adenauer's own chief of staff, Hans Globke, had co-written an official legal commentary on the Nazis' Nuremberg race laws.

25
00:05:20,601 --> 00:05:26,639
Gehlen himself was famously secretive, and rarely allowed himself to be photographed.

26
00:05:27,739 --> 00:05:43,077
Historians later commissioned by the BND found that his organisation had also spied on West German politicians, including Adenauer's opponents, the Social Democrats, and passed what it learned to the chancellor's office.

27
00:05:44,177 --> 00:06:05,020
CIA documents released in 2006 showed that in 1958, West German intelligence told the CIA that Adolf Eichmann, a chief organiser of the Holocaust, was living in Argentina under a false name. Israeli agents captured him there in 1960.

28
00:06:07,220 --> 00:06:19,273
Hiring former SS men had a cost. Heinz Felfe, a former SS officer, rose to a senior post in the BND's counter-intelligence, hunting Soviet spies.

29
00:06:20,373 --> 00:06:32,960
He was a Soviet spy himself, working for the KGB. By the time he was arrested in 1961, he had been betraying agents and operations for about ten years.

30
00:06:34,060 --> 00:06:47,884
In 1963 he was sentenced to fourteen years. Another former SS man in the service, Hans Clemens, was convicted alongside him. The scandal badly damaged Gehlen's standing.

31
00:06:48,984 --> 00:06:58,691
In 1969 Felfe was released in a prisoner exchange with East Germany, where he later became a university professor.

32
00:06:59,791 --> 00:07:11,908
Critics argued that men with hidden Nazi pasts were easy targets for Soviet recruiters. The policy that gave Gehlen experienced staff also left his service exposed.

33
00:07:14,108 --> 00:07:21,169
Gehlen retired in 1968, his reputation dented by the Felfe affair.

34
00:07:22,269 --> 00:07:39,699
In 1971 he published his memoirs, The Service. In them he claimed that Martin Bormann, Hitler's private secretary, had been a Soviet agent. Historians reject the claim. Bormann died in Berlin in 1945.

35
00:07:40,799 --> 00:07:50,100
Gehlen died at his home in Bavaria in 1979, aged seventy-seven. He was never charged with any crime.

36
00:07:52,300 --> 00:08:07,041
In 1998, the US Congress ordered the release of secret files on Nazi war criminals. Historians found that American intelligence had used or protected dozens of them, many through the Org.

37
00:08:08,141 --> 00:08:22,925
In 2011 the BND itself opened its archives to independent historians. Their studies confirmed that former SS, SD and Gestapo men had been recruited in considerable numbers.

38
00:08:24,025 --> 00:08:34,692
The service itself survived him. Today the BND works under parliamentary oversight, and since 2019 its headquarters have been in Berlin.

39
00:08:35,792 --> 00:08:47,739
His defenders said he was a professional soldier, not a Nazi, who gave the West intelligence it badly needed at the start of the Cold War, and built a service that still exists.

40
00:08:48,839 --> 00:09:01,041
His critics say his organisation gave men implicated in Nazi crimes a comfortable new career, shielded them from justice, and let Soviet spies in through the same door.

41
00:09:02,141 --> 00:09:12,360
Gehlen's story is not about one man's crimes. It is about a bargain: that in the Cold War, the past could be overlooked if the intelligence was good enough.

42
00:09:13,460 --> 00:09:26,964
The bargain bought less than it promised. The service built to keep secrets from Moscow was riddled with Moscow's spies. And the price was paid by victims whose persecutors were never brought to justice.
